# The domain layer

The business's concepts and rules, as plain TypeScript. Nothing here knows it
runs in React, in a browser, or on Databricks. The reasons are in
`docs/design/architecture.md`.

## Imports

Nothing from React, AppKit, a chart library, any other folder of the client, or
the browser (`window`, `document`, `fetch`, storage). Other domain files and
small pure libraries only.

## Objects never change

React only re-renders when it sees a different object, and the React Compiler
counts on nothing it holds being changed in place. So:

- Fields are `readonly`. No setters.
- A method that "changes" an object returns a new one:

  ```ts
  // Wrong: mutates an object that React may already hold.
  order.status = 'shipped';

  // Right: a new Order, and the old one stays as it was.
  const shipped = order.withStatus('shipped');
  ```

- Collections are `ReadonlyArray` and `ReadonlyMap`, and are copied, never
  pushed to.

## Classes

Classes are welcome here, and only here; the rest of the client is functions.

- Name the concept, never the role: `Invoice`, `ShippingRate`, not
  `InvoiceManager`, `RateHandler` or `OrderProcessor`. A name ending in an
  agent noun means behavior is missing from the object it acts on.
- Put behavior with the data it uses. A class with only fields and a separate
  service full of logic is the anemic model this layer avoids.
- One class per file, named after the class.
- When behavior varies by kind, give each kind its own class (polymorphism or a
  strategy) instead of branching on a type field. A `switch` over a
  discriminated union is fine where only the data varies, as long as it is
  exhaustive.

## Documentation and tests

- Every export has a JSDoc comment saying what it represents in the business.
- Tests run in Node, below the UI: one example per rule, plus property tests
  with `fast-check` for rules that hold across many inputs. They must kill every
  mutant that mutation testing makes.

## When the server needs these rules

Move this folder to `shared/domain/` so the client and the server import one
copy. `docs/design/architecture.md` says how.
